1. Print DSA for five times in a loop
#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    for(int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    {
        printf("DSA\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
Output:
DSA
DSA
DSA
DSA
DSA
2. Checking for loop in various cases
If remove i = 0
for(; i < 5; i++)
It will show no output because i has not been declared or initialized elsewhere. The program will give an error or behave unexpectedly.
If remove i < 5
for(int i = 0; ; i++)
The loop runs infinitely, printing DSA forever because the condition is omitted, so it is treated as always true.
If remove i++
for(int i = 0; i < 5; )
If initialization, condition and increment parts are omitted, it becomes equivalent to:
while()
The condition is considered always true, so the loop never ends. The loop runs infinitely, printing DSA forever.
